refuted.why:
Factually wrong about the code, verified by driving it. The claim is that the sentence "exists for the mouse and for the press, not for the name" and that a screen-reader user hears "Home, button, dimmed and nothing else." It does not. `title` on an element whose name comes from `aria-label` is not discarded — per accname it falls through to the DESCRIPTION computation, and Chrome exposes it. Pulling the full AX tree over CDP at 1440x960 on `?v=paper&state=tasks.board` returns exactly this for the four doors the seat names, plus the mark:

  {name:"Home", desc:"Every project in one list. Not here yet.", disabled=true, focusable=true}
  {name:"Inbox", desc:"What came in while you were working. Not here yet.", ...}
  {name:"Help and settings", desc:"Guides and support. Not here yet.", ...}
  {name:"Orla, venue manager", desc:"Your account, in Signal Studio. Not here yet.", ...}
  {name:"Signal Studio", desc:"Every Signal Studio app in one place. Not here yet.", ...}

Accessible descriptions are announced on focus by NVDA (Report object descriptions is on by default), by JAWS, and as a hint by VoiceOver. So the sentence reaches assistive tech on arrival at the door, without pressing. The seat inspected the DOM attributes and inferred the computation instead of reading the tree.

The press channel is also better than described. `_gate-suite.html:7925-7936` intercepts `[data-rail]` clicks, and a real `<button>` with `aria-disabled` (not `disabled`) fires click from both keys. Driven: Enter on Home puts "Every project in one list. Not here yet." in `#say` (role=status, aria-live=polite, one region for the whole suite, never replaced) and leaves focus on `home`; Space on the account tile puts "Your account, in Signal Studio. Not here yet." there. Focus survives the repaint via `paintRail`'s save/restore at 7721-7731.

The proposed fix is worse than the current state, and its own wording is what breaks it. "aria-label='Home. Every project in one list. Not here yet.' keeping title as it is for the hover" leaves `title` in place — so `title` still resolves as the description, and Chrome would speak the sentence TWICE on a single focus stop: "Home. Every project in one list. Not here yet., button, unavailable. Every project in one list. Not here yet." Press it and the live region says it a third time. Three utterances of one sentence in one interaction is a regression from one.

It also breaks two things the spine already gets right. First, voice control: Dragon and Voice Access target a control by its accessible name, and "click Home" stops matching when the name is an eleven-word sentence. Second, the rail's own naming convention at 7695-7699 — live tiles are named "Notes"/"Timeline" and the active one "Tasks, the page you are on". Giving the four dead doors sentence-length names while the three live products keep one-word names makes the doors that do nothing the loudest, longest stops on the roving-tabindex walk of the spine. That inverts the emphasis of the object the suite is built around.

Severity is overstated. As written this reads as blocking ("the product will not do what it says" to a screen-reader user); the actual state is that the sentence is delivered on focus and again on press, so nothing is wrong. Refinement at most.

